Well, I *might* be wrong about this, but this is the way I have heard the
experiment proposed:

There is a box. Inside is a cat, a single radioactive particle and a poisonous
gas in a bottle. The decay of a single particle is completely random - thus it
has a probability wavefunction. If the particle decays, then (somehow) the gas
in the bottle is released killing the cat. HOWEVER, the lid of the box has a
tripwire on it - opening the box will also release the gas and kill the cat.
The point is this: If you open the box, the cat is always dead. But you don't
know whether it died from the particle decaying or from opening the box.

This is tied into one of the basic postulates of Quantum Mechanics. Expressed
simply, it is: Making an observation (opening the box) affects the measured
quantity (the cat). The longer you wait, the higher the probability the cat was
killed by the particle and not by the observation. A practical example of this
is measuring the position and the velocity of a particle. The greater your
knowledge of the position of the particle, the more uncertain you are about the
particles velocity (and vice versa). It is never possible to know exactly the
position and the velocity of a particle.

And I thought my undergrad Quantum Mech. would never be useful...

